# Build the hardware simulator and dry run

## Scope and existing evidence

Visualize pen-up travel, pen-down paths, limits, time, pauses, and checkpoints
without connecting to a physical machine.

Current executable dry-run evidence now binds one exact device-neutral plan to
one concrete coordinate-calibration record and one ordered limit-evidence entry
per plan operation. It reuses the plan domain's structural operation kind rather
than defining a second pen-up/pen-down/pause/checkpoint vocabulary.

Motion cannot mark limit checking inapplicable, and unknown or violated limits
reject at the exact operation index. A 16-case matrix covers all four limit
states at pen-up, pause, checkpoint, and pen-down operations. Coordinate
transforms, limit geometry, visual rendering, timing simulation beyond plan
evidence, and hardware contact remain open.

Offline simulation now also produces a borrowed inspection trace only after the
complete dry run validates. Trace construction runs the same 16-state matrix and
must either return the identical typed dry-run failure or borrow the exact
admitted package with aligned limit evidence. Six compact under/over-count
fixtures additionally preserve evaluation-count mismatch before limit-state
inspection. The trace retains the exact plan
and calibration, operation order, pen-up/down geometry, pause/checkpoint
payloads, per-operation limit evidence, plan/revision identities, physical
bounds, and admitted total estimated duration without copying or rewriting plan
authority.

The validated trace is constructor-owned and exposes only read-only dry-run and
step views, so callers cannot fabricate a successful trace around an invalid
dry run.
Pixel/vector visualization, coordinate transforms, limit geometry, and derived
per-operation timing remain open.

## Finite offline dry-run storyboard

**Inputs:** an immutable plan identity and accepted revision; physical page
bounds `210 x 297 mm`, writable bounds `x=20..190, y=20..277 mm`;
caller-supplied registered calibration identity; and one explicit limit state
for each ordered operation. **Output:** an offline trace with each operation's
kind, endpoints, contact state, bounds result, pause/checkpoint markers, and
admitted duration if already known. No serial, USB, network, or motor command
is emitted; no estimated duration is invented.

**One-page worked trace:** operation 0 lifts and travels to `(30,40) mm`;
operation 1 deposits one title stroke `(30,40)` to `(60,40) mm`;
operation 2 checkpoints completion of operation 1; operation 3 is a declared
operator pause; operation 4 remains pen-up and travels from `(60,40)` to
`(30,55) mm`; operation 5 deposits `(30,55)` to `(65,55) mm`. For all six
steps, the caller's boundary status is `WithinLimits`, and the read-only
trace preserves each original motion, pause, and checkpoint. A future visual
layer distinguishes pen-up as dashed travel, pen-down as solid ink, pause as
a hold marker, checkpoint as a numbered marker, and bounds as a rectangle;
no pixel renderer is claimed here. A timestamp or total duration is shown
only if the input plan supplied accepted timing evidence; otherwise `Unknown`.

**Refusal:** if operation 5 instead deposits from `(30,55)` to `(194,55) mm`,
its endpoint exceeds the writable right edge, so caller-supplied limit
validation must return `OutOfBounds` at operation 5. No complete trace or
simulated-success receipt is produced. A missing operation limit status,
changed plan revision, stale calibration, or mismatched evidence count also
refuses rather than drawing a misleading safe path.

**Invariants:** no physical channel is opened; the trace is a projection, not
a replanned sequence; original order, geometry, owner IDs and checkpoint
references remain identical. Bounds must be checked for pen-up and pen-down,
not only inked points. A trace cannot mark an unverified limit as safe.

**Assumptions and risks:** the backend has read-only dry-run and trace
structures with explicit limit states. The caller still must compute actual
coordinate transforms, envelope limits and timings. No viewport renderer,
measured acceleration, collision dynamics, automatic limit evaluation, or
physical hardware test is implemented by this document.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** theoretical for the six-event visual storyboard, 2026-11-02;
  practical only for the existing typed read-only limit/trace admission.
- **Delivered:** a one-page pen-up/down/checkpoint/pause sequence and one
  out-of-bounds typed refusal with no device operation.
- **Unavailable:** physical motion simulation, rendered pixels, automatic
  geometry limits, calibrated timing, and any actuator authority.
